% global_cardinality rule (a) (M7-T24, D-0099): a SATURATED value is removed, as an
% interior HOLE, from every other variable of the scope.
%
% Exactly one of x, y, z, w takes 2. The search guesses x = 2 first (indomain_median),
% which saturates the value: gcc punches 2 out of y, z and w, strictly inside each
% window -- the inference std's int_eq_reif/bool2int/int_lin_eq decomposition makes and
% gcc did not (D-0097). int_lin_ne then excludes y = 1 (settling y's lower bound OVER
% gcc's hole, so the settle line cites it) and y = 3, and the branch fails. The refutation
% of x = 2 therefore rests on the hole's line, and that line is NOT RUP against the .opb
% alone: z and w both still hold 2 inside their windows, so the counting row keeps slack
% (test/unit/test_trace.ml pins it and checks the break). SAT, so the proof's lines are
% evidence rather than vacuous (D-0053, D-0066/D-0073).
var 1..3: x :: output_var;
var 1..3: y :: output_var;
var 1..3: z :: output_var;
var 1..3: w :: output_var;
constraint fzn_global_cardinality([x,y,z,w],[2],[1]);
constraint int_lin_ne([1,1],[x,y],3);
constraint int_lin_ne([1,1],[x,y],5);
solve :: int_search([x,y,z,w], input_order, indomain_median, complete) satisfy;
